    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "We have been studying the Yehoshafat fault, a normal fault within the central Shlomo Graben located at about 10 km west-northwest of the town of Elat in southern Israel. Last year, we presented our preliminary findings on the geology and structure of the Yehoshafat fault (see Aydin and Eyal, RIP Workshop1995, VolumeVI). The fault has a trace length of about 2kin and is marked by what remains of a formation made up of a 60 m lower shale unit, 25 m of middle carbonates, and 35 m of upper shale. Both shale units have been stretched over a planar discontinuity defined by the footwall cut-off planes of the underlying sandstone and limestone units for about 250m, the magnitude of the normal slip. Thus, the fault geometry and the position of the shale units reveal a smearing process by which the shale units reduce their thickness or nearly vanish by thinning perpendicular to the fault and stretching parallel to the fault. The lower shale unit is reduced from 60 m to a thickness less than 0.5m in a few exposures. The middle carbonates display boudinage and form discontinuous lenses along the fault.\r\n\r\nIn the past year, we improved upon the geologic map of the fault and our understanding of the fault zone architecture. We developed a generalized conceptualmodelof a normalfault with shale smear. This model forms the basis for simulating fluid withdrawal from a sandstone reservoir (the Nubian Sandstone) located at the footwall of the fault in order to evaluate the influence of a physically and geologically realistic fault zone with shale smearon hydrocarbonwithdrawal. Also tested is the impact of the secondary faults and joints associated with the fault. The results displayed in this poster presentation show that the pressure field mimics the distribution of the shale unit both in its normal stratigraphic place as cap rocks and in the fault zone as shale smear. A model with the secondary geological structures such as small faults, joints and limestone lenses in and around the fault zone shows that these structures havea significant impactonthe fluid flow andthe pressuredistribution.",
